\subsubsection{Experimental results}

Our tests generate random weighted graphs of size $n$ by placing $n$ points randomly on $[0,1]$. We then choose a threshold distance $l$, the distance between two points $a$ and $b$ is of course $|a-b|$ and we create the graph by composing the distance with the function:
\[\fonction {f_l} {\mathbb R^+} {\bar{\mathbb R}} a {\frac{l^2-a^2}{la}}\]

This function was chosen because of three intuitive properties we expected from the weight: $f_l(0)=+\infty$, $f_l(l)=0$ and $\underset{x\to+\infty}{\lim}f_l(x)=-\infty$.

The values of the threshold distance $l$ will determine the edge density of the graph and therefore the size $c$ of the clusters. All the results in the following table are average results on 1,000 measures on graphs of 10,000 vertices. Those results aim at showing the speed (through the number of $\opt'$ values it computes) and the accuracy (through the cost of the answer it returns) of both heuristics but also the impact of the size of the clusters.

